{"type":"user","message":{"content":"# Generate Fix Prompt for an Unbound Scenario Step\n\n## Reference Documentation\n- Read(`./docs/architecture/architecture.yaml`) — architecture_yaml\n\n## Where the fix goes\n\nRead the architectural spec above and find the\n`architecture.testing.suites[]` entry whose `service:` equals the\nsubject's **Service**. That entry's `path:` is the suite root, and the\nonly tree the applier may write:\n\n    \u003csuite path\u003e/steps/\n\nGlob and Read every file already there before proposing anything. The\ndefinitions in it share one state type and a set of helpers; a fix that\nre-implements what a sibling already does is the drift this command\nexists to prevent.\n\n## Subject — Registry Scenario\n\n**Scenario ID:** INT-900\n**Description:** MCP /mcp endpoint responds 200 to a valid initialize POST\n**Service:** mcp-service\n**Requirement:** fixture seed — expects `build tests` to write the step definitions\n\n### Steps\nGiven:\n  - the MCP server is running on its configured port\nWhen:\n  - the Claude User posts a valid JSON-RPC initialize request to /mcp\nThen:\n  - the server returns HTTP 200\n\n\n## Validation Failure\n\nThe following check failed and needs to be fixed:\n\n### Failed Check: build-tests/implementation\n\n**Question:** Does every step of this scenario resolve to a step definition\nin the suite that owns it?\n\nFirst find the suite. Read the architectural spec listed under\nReference Documentation and look through\n`architecture.testing.suites[]` for the entry whose `service:`\nequals this scenario's **Service**. That entry's `path:` is\nthe suite's root; its step definitions live under\n`\u003cpath\u003e/steps/`.\n\nThen bind each step. A step definition is a call of the form\n`suite.Step(`\u003cregexp\u003e`, \u003cfunc\u003e)`. A step RESOLVES when exactly\none registered regexp matches its text — the text alone, with\nthe Given/When/Then/And keyword stripped, because a definition\nis bound by wording and not by which block a step sits in.\n\nOne exception, and it is not a loophole: a step whose text\nopens with `llm:` or `judge:` is run by a MODEL rather than by\na step definition. It always resolves and it must never be\ngiven one — a definition matching `judge: …` would bind a\nclause written precisely because no pattern could settle it.\nMark such a step MODEL-RUN and move on.\n\nPass when every step of the scenario resolves.\nFail when any step resolves to none, or to more than one.\n\nContext: emit one line per step, then a verdict.\n  - \"step: \u003cstep text\u003e -\u003e \u003cmatching pattern, MODEL-RUN, or UNDEFINED\u003e\"\n  - \"Verdict: executable / \u003cN\u003e step(s) unbound\"\n\n**Actual:** fail\n\n**Suggested Fix Template:**\nAuthor the missing step definition(s) in the suite that owns\nthis scenario.\n\nSteps:\n  1. Read the architectural spec and find the\n     `architecture.testing.suites[]` entry whose `service:`\n     matches this scenario's. Its `path:` is the suite root.\n  2. Read the existing definitions under `\u003csuite path\u003e/steps/`\n     to learn the state type they share and the helpers\n     already available. REUSE them — a new definition that\n     re-implements what a sibling already does is the drift\n     this command exists to prevent.\n  3. For each unbound step, register one definition:\n     `suite.Step(`^\u003canchored regexp\u003e$`, \u003cfunc\u003e)`. Put a\n     capture group where the step's text varies — a quoted\n     run, a number — so one definition serves every scenario\n     phrasing it that way, and do not write a definition whose\n     pattern is a literal copy of one scenario's line.\n  4. Implement the function against the suite's state type.\n     Given prepares, When acts, Then asserts and returns an\n     error naming what it expected and what it got.\n  5. Do not weaken an existing definition to make this step\n     match. Do not edit the registry.\n  6. Never write a definition for a step whose text opens with\n     `llm:` or `judge:`. Those are run by a model by the\n     scenario author's explicit choice, and a pattern that\n     captured one would take that choice back.\n\n## Your Task\nAnalyze if you have enough context to generate a confident fix.\n\n**If you can generate a confident fix**, output a fix prompt the applier\ncan execute. It may Write a new file or Edit an existing one under\n`\u003csuite path\u003e/steps/` and nowhere else. The fix MUST:\n- Register one definition per unbound step:\n  `suite.Step(`^\u003canchored regexp\u003e$`, \u003cfunc\u003e)`, added to the suite's\n  existing `Register` function rather than a new one.\n- Put a capture group where the step's text varies — a quoted run, a\n  number — so the definition serves every scenario phrasing it that\n  way. A pattern that is a literal copy of one scenario's line is a\n  definition that will never match a second scenario.\n- Implement the function against the state type the sibling definitions\n  already use: Given prepares, When acts, Then asserts and returns an\n  error naming what it expected and what it got.\n- Never weaken an existing definition so this step matches it.\n- Never modify the scenario registry.\n\n=== FILE_START: tmp/2026-08-31-11-55-01-90387/01-INT-900-fix-prompts.md ===\n# Fix Prompt for Scenario INT-900: MCP /mcp endpoint responds 200 to a valid initialize POST\n\n## Target\n`\u003csuite path\u003e/steps/\u003cfile\u003e.go` — the suite resolved from the\narchitectural spec for service `mcp-service`.\n\n## Required Changes\n### Change #N: [description]\n**File:** \u003crepo-relative path\u003e\n**Action:** Use the Write tool (new file) or the Edit tool (extend\nexisting) with the exact content below.\n**Content / new_string:**\n```\n\u003cpaste the exact registration and function here\u003e\n```\n**old_string** (only for Edit on an existing file):\n```\n\u003cverbatim slice the new code should replace or append after\u003e\n```\n=== FILE_END: tmp/2026-08-31-11-